1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:04,380
After seeing two examples that are not valid,  

2
00:00:04,380 --> 00:00:10,800
let's study one that is: Peterson's algorithm 
(the check-order & competition approach).

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:17,880
In this case, we have one shared 
turn variable and two flag variables,  

4
00:00:17,880 --> 00:00:24,420
one for each thread. The variable turn is 
set to A and both flags are set to false. 

5
00:00:24,420 --> 00:00:28,500
In the entry section, thread 
A sets its flag to true,  

6
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:34,920
sets turn to B, and then waits until 
turn value is B and B's flag is true. 

7
00:00:35,820 --> 00:00:41,340
Once A exits the critical section, 
it sets its flag to false. 

8
00:00:41,340 --> 00:00:45,600
Let's see why this approach is valid, 
and let's start with mutual exclusion.

9
00:00:46,680 --> 00:00:49,440
We consider the same setup we considered before,  

10
00:00:49,440 --> 00:00:55,200
and we check if A can enter its critical 
section while B is in its critical section. 

11
00:00:55,980 --> 00:01:01,620
What we can say is that, before entering 
its critical section, A needs to exit the  

12
00:01:01,620 --> 00:01:08,520
while loop. This can happen in 2 cases: 
if flagB is false or if turn is set to A. 

13
00:01:09,180 --> 00:01:13,320
Let's look at the first case, 
let's say flagB is false. 

14
00:01:15,180 --> 00:01:21,600
For B to enter its critical section, it needs 
to set flagB to true (first instruction of  

15
00:01:21,600 --> 00:01:27,780
the entry section). Since we are considering 
the case in which A sees flagB set to false,  

16
00:01:27,780 --> 00:01:35,280
it means in this case B would set flagB to 
true after A sees flagB being false (we use  

17
00:01:35,280 --> 00:01:41,640
an arrow connecting event X to event 
Y to denote that X happened before Y). 

18
00:01:43,320 --> 00:01:49,200
After setting its flag to 
true, B sets turn to idA. 

19
00:01:50,640 --> 00:01:53,340
Then B will enter the while loop.  

20
00:01:53,940 --> 00:02:01,440
Will B exit it? Not until it sees flagA 
being false, but that will only happen  

21
00:02:01,440 --> 00:02:08,640
after A exits its critical section, which means
we cannot find a counterexample in this case.

22
00:02:10,920 --> 00:02:17,100
We are left with the second case, 
let's say A sees the turn value is idA. 

23
00:02:17,100 --> 00:02:24,840
Let's resume our previous example.
A sets flagA to true, then A sets  

24
00:02:24,840 --> 00:02:33,000
turn to idB, and finally, A sees turn is 
equal to idA and thus exits the while loop. 

25
00:02:34,020 --> 00:02:39,120
Notice in this case the value 
of turn was set to B by A  

26
00:02:39,660 --> 00:02:45,480
in the second instruction of the entry 
section, but then B must have set it to  

27
00:02:45,480 --> 00:02:52,060
A for A to read its id during the execution 
of the third instruction of the entry set. 

28
00:02:52,620 --> 00:03:02,220
After setting turn to idA B enters its while 
loop. Can it exit it and enter its critical  

29
00:03:02,220 --> 00:03:11,940
section before A? No, because turn is set to 
idA and flagA will remain true until A exits  

30
00:03:11,940 --> 00:03:16,920
its critical section. So, also in this 
case, we cannot find a counter-example.

31
00:03:18,540 --> 00:03:23,100
As for previous algorithms, you can switch A and B  

32
00:03:23,100 --> 00:03:29,140
and show B cannot enter its critical 
section while A is running its own.

33
00:03:29,760 --> 00:03:35,040
Now that we know mutual execution 
is guaranteed, let's check progress. 

34
00:03:35,940 --> 00:03:39,720
Can it happen that one thread wants 
to enter its critical section,  

35
00:03:39,720 --> 00:03:46,320
but it has to wait for unbounded time while the 
other thread is not in its critical section? 

36
00:03:47,160 --> 00:03:52,920
Can A want to enter its critical 
section and be waiting for B even  

37
00:03:52,920 --> 00:04:02,040
though B is not in its critical section?
It is easy to show this is not possible: if A is  

38
00:04:02,040 --> 00:04:08,880
waiting, then B can enter its critical section 
because flagB is true and turn value is idB.  

39
00:04:09,540 --> 00:04:16,860
But if B is in its reminder section, then flagB 
is false, which means A can enter its section. 

40
00:04:17,880 --> 00:04:21,600
Similarly, if B is waiting, 
then A can enter its critical  

41
00:04:21,600 --> 00:04:28,200
section because flagA is true and 
turn value is idA or B can enter  

42
00:04:28,200 --> 00:04:34,980
its own because A set flagA to false 
before entering its reminder section

43
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:43,860
We are now left with fairness. In this case, 
we are going to look into bounded waiting. 

44
00:04:43,860 --> 00:04:49,620
Is it possible for one thread, let's say 
B, to wait to enter its critical section  

45
00:04:49,620 --> 00:04:54,300
while A manages to enter its critical 
section an unbounded number of times? 

46
00:04:54,900 --> 00:04:58,440
Let's try to find an example of this happening. 

47
00:04:58,440 --> 00:05:03,240
In this case, A is running its 
critical section and B is waiting. 

48
00:05:04,800 --> 00:05:11,160
What we are trying to find is an execution in 
which A enters again its critical section while  

49
00:05:11,160 --> 00:05:16,740
B still waits. If this can happen once, then 
it can happen an unbounded number of times. 

50
00:05:18,240 --> 00:05:22,980
After exiting its critical 
section, A sets flagA to false. 

51
00:05:24,300 --> 00:05:30,480
A runs its reminder section and then starts 
the loop again by setting flagA to true. 

52
00:05:31,800 --> 00:05:36,660
Then, A sets turn to idB 
and enters the while loop. 

53
00:05:37,500 --> 00:05:46,320
If B is waiting in its loop, then flagB is true. 
The value of turn is idB (because A just set it). 

54
00:05:46,320 --> 00:05:51,960
Then, we can conclude A cannot 
exit the loop until B is done  

55
00:05:51,960 --> 00:05:55,260
with its critical section. 
Hence, this cannot happen. 

56
00:05:55,920 --> 00:05:59,820
As before, swap A with B, and 
the same reasoning applies. 

57
00:05:59,820 --> 00:06:04,620
So, after all this, we can conclude 
that Peterson's algorithm is valid!

58
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:10,920
Before moving to the next part of the lecture, 
please have a look at these links about  

59
00:06:10,920 --> 00:06:16,740
counterexamples and proofs by way of contradiction 
if you are not familiar with these concepts.

